<p>To use this page you accept that every result you request is recorded in an audit log. The record holds what you enter (names, birth date, time and place, the place where you are now if you enter it, including the details of the person you love in Soul Affinity (Love)) and a summary of the result. Opening a link that someone shared with you is not recorded again. The one exception is a link with hidden details, described below.</p>
<p>It does not hold your IP address, and nothing is used to track you. Your acceptance is remembered with one small functional cookie that contains no identifier. The recorded data is kept by the site owner and can be removed on request.</p>
<p>If your browser allows it, this page remembers, in your browser only, the details you enter for yourself (Self Discovery), a short list of the people you looked up (names, birth dates and times, cities and current cities) and a list of the hidden codes other people shared with you, each with a nickname you choose, so you do not have to type them again. A short-lived, non-identifying note may also be kept in the tab (session storage) and disappears when the tab is closed. A hidden code is stored as it was received; the page never shows the details inside it. This stays on your device and never leaves it through this feature; it is sent to the site only when you submit a form, like anything you type. Use &lsquo;Clear data&rsquo; to erase all of this; withdrawing your acceptance erases it too.</p>
<p>You can create a link or QR code that holds your own details (name, birth date, time and place) so that another person can compare themselves with you. To create it, your browser sends those details once to this site, which does not store or log them when it makes the link. The page of the person who opens it does not display your details, but &lsquo;hidden&rsquo; only means not shown on that person&rsquo;s screen: the details are inside the link and can be read by anyone who decodes or intercepts it, so share it only with people you trust. When someone opens a link with hidden details, the full details of both people, the one who opens it and the one who shared it, are recorded in the audit log like any other request. Links hold the details in the address, so they can also appear in browser history and in server access logs.</p>
